# Technology Stack & Dependency Discipline

This rule defines the default architecture and dependency policy for ZZZKidz.
It complements `.cursor/rules/zzzkidz-project-standards.mdc` and must not rewrite or replace that rulebook.

## Approved Default Stack

Use this stack as the default architecture unless the user explicitly overrides it:

| Concern | Technology |
|---|---|
| Framework | Next.js (App Router) |
| Language | TypeScript |
| Styling | Tailwind CSS |
| UI components | shadcn/ui |
| Accessible primitives | Radix UI (when required) |
| Animation | Framer Motion (premium, restrained) |
| Icons | Lucide Icons |
| CMS | Sanity CMS |
| Images | `next/image` for standard image optimization |
| Video / media-heavy workflows | Cloudinary (primary) |
| App deployment | Vercel |
| Domain / DNS | GoDaddy only |
| Runtime validation | Zod (when required) |
| E2E tests | Playwright (important flows) |
| Unit tests | Vitest (where appropriate) |
| Analytics | Google Analytics 4 |
| Search | Google Search Console |
| Tag management | Google Tag Manager |
| Ads / remarketing | Meta Pixel; Google Ads conversion tracking where required |

## Tech-Stack Rules

- Prefer native Next.js/browser functionality before introducing another dependency.
- Before installing any package, check whether the existing stack already solves the problem.
- Do not introduce duplicate libraries with overlapping responsibilities.
- Keep client-side JavaScript to the minimum necessary.
- Prefer Server Components unless client-side interactivity is required.
- Keep the architecture fully compatible with Vercel.
- Use environment variables for configuration and secrets.
- Never expose private credentials or secrets to client-side code.

Do not substitute alternate libraries for the approved stack without explicit instruction
(e.g. do not swap Framer Motion for another animation library, or Sanity for another CMS).

## Technology Selection & Dependency Discipline

Before introducing any new library, framework, SDK, service, or dependency:

1. Check the existing codebase for an existing solution.
2. Check whether Next.js, TypeScript, Tailwind, browser APIs, or the current stack can solve the requirement.
3. Only introduce a new dependency when there is a clear technical justification.
4. Prefer lightweight, mature, well-maintained dependencies.
5. Consider performance, SEO, accessibility, security, maintainability, and Vercel compatibility before adding it.
6. Do not add a dependency merely for convenience.
7. Do not create multiple implementations of the same functionality.
8. Reuse and extend existing project abstractions wherever practical.

## Enforcement

- Generate future code consistently with this stack and this discipline.
- If a requested change would violate this rule or the ZZZKidz project standards, flag the conflict before proceeding.
- Do not modify unrelated project rules or architecture merely to apply this directive.
